<title>JAPAN: Japan says will not seek price cut on Canada grain.</title>
<headline>Japan says will not seek price cut on Canada grain.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1997-02-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Japan's Food Agency and trade houses said on Thursday they will not ask Canada to cut wheat export prices because of shipment delays.</p>
<p>Philippine firms earlier asked Canadian wheat suppliers to reduce prices on orders contracted for delivery this month due to supply delays.</p>
<p>&quot;The Philippines may have its own style, but we're not going to ask for price cuts because of their shipment delays,&quot; an official of the Food Agency said.</p>
<p>Japan, one of the world's largest wheat importers, buys mainly from the United States, Canada and Australia.</p>
<p>Weather and transportation problems have slowed shipments of wheat to Canadian ports over the past several weeks. On Tuesday, 39 ships were waiting to load grains and oilseeds on Canada's Pacific Coast.</p>
<p>-- Tokyo Commodities Desk +81-3 3432-7391</p>
